ted on the x-axis HS f1  expression over expression is greater  and more pronounced across this range of  cancers than is seem ik so it's becoming  very apparent that cancer cells depend  on HS f 1 for growth viability and  survival and that a targeted agent that  results in increasing the activity of HS  f1 is is not going to promote an  anti-cancer response we wondered what  the mechanism behind hsp90 effects on HS  f 1 is because understanding the  mechanism may allow us to intercede and  and block the effects of hsp90 so the  current model that's been in the  literature for many years is that hsp90  binds to monomers of h s f1 in  unstressed cells and in the presence of  misfolded proteins caused by proteotoxic  stress such as heat shock hsp90 comes  off of ages of 1 to deal with the  misfolded proteins and allows HS f1 to  trimer eyes which it must do to become  an active transcription factor  so how do i chess p90 inhibitors affect  the hs8 HSR or heat shock response based  on this model the concept was that hsp90  inhibitors by preventing ATP from  binding to hsp90 caused dissociation of  hsp90 from monomeric HS f1 and similarly  to heat shock allow HS f1 to trimer eyes  and become an active transcription  factor so the dogma in the literature is  that hsp90 functions as a repressor of  HS f1 activation it functions as a  sensor of proteotoxic stress and release  of HS f one from hsp90 is a key event if  not the key event in induction of the  heat shock response so we asked  ourselves does this model make sense and  in fact we came to the conclusion that  it does not first of all it's extremely  difficult to visualize the interaction  of endogenous hsp90 and HS f1 in cells  without chemical cross-linking and a  recent study in fact in vitro showed  that the constitutive isoform of hsp90  actually promotes temperature dependent  try memorization of HS f1 inconsistent  with the current model so we decided to  examine more closely if we could detect  a stable in