cule directly to tumor cells  and maintaining it at relatively high  concentrations in tumor cells for an  extended time because of its attachment  to hsp90 might not only improve its  efficacy toward tumor inhibition or  killing but also reduce its systemic  toxicity and this has in fact proved to  be the case in collaboration with Lee  Hellman and Kristine Heskey in the  pediatric oncology branch here at NCI  we've looked at use of this conjugate in  animal models of Ewing sarcoma and  rhabdomyosarcoma to pediatric sarcomas  that are frequently treated with the  reno t kin but invariably stopped  responding after in a certain time shown  in these graphs are the responses of a  rhabdomyosarcoma line rh 32 either hsp90  inhibitors alone which is can attest but  in this case shown and the red line in  panel a 2 arena t can alone shown in the  purple line and two three different  doses of this conjugate that I've  discussed shown in the yellow  blue and dark purple lines as you can  see hsp90 inhibitors itself is  ineffective in inhibiting the growth of  our age 30 Sina graphs in mice while  arena T can although initially very  effective eventually loses activity and  the tumors return in contrast the drug  conjugate in a dose-dependent manner is  able to significantly prolong regression  of these tumors the result is even more  dramatic in a Ewing sarcoma model shown  in panel B where you can see again that  can attest boob alone the hsp90  inhibitors is ineffective arre no T can  as expected is very effective but it's  it's anti-cancer activity is transient  and again there's a dose-dependent  effect of the conjugate such that at a  doses at doses that are achievable in  humans there is essentially no  recurrence of this cancer after two  treatments this is also observed in a  Ewing sarcoma model subjected to  protracted therapy with arena t can  compared to the conjugate as you can see  even though arena t can treatment is  protracted two tumors eventually will  re